    "extracted text": "The paper examines Al-Cu 206 cast alloys with varying Fe, Si, and Mn contents to evaluate the effect of iron-rich intermetallics on tensile properties. The alloys are designated with a three-digit code where the first digit represents the approximate Fe content (B for baseline at 0.15% Fe), and the second and third digits represent Si and Mn contents respectively. The alloys were heat treated in two conditions: T4 (solution treatment followed by natural aging) and T7 (solution treatment followed by artificial overaging). Solution treatment was performed at 525°C for 8 hours, followed by water quenching at 60°C. T4 condition involved natural aging at room temperature for 7 days after solution treatment. T7 condition involved artificial aging at 185°C for 5 hours after solution treatment.",
    "property mapping": "Alloy designation: B11, B33, 311, 333, 511, 515, 533 (experimental Al-Cu 206 cast alloys)\nTemper: T4 (solution treatment + natural aging), T7 (solution treatment + artificial overaging)\nUTS: Ultimate Tensile Strength in MPa\nYS: Yield Strength at 0.2% offset strain in MPa\nEl/elong: Elongation percentage",
    "abbreviations": "B11, B33, 311, 333, 511, 515, 533: Experimental alloy designations where the first digit stands for the approximate content of Fe (B for baseline at a maximum specification limit of 0.15% Fe). The second and third digits stand for Si and Mn contents, respectively.\nT4: Solution treatment followed by natural aging (525°C for 8 hours + water quenching at 60°C + aging at room temperature for 7 days)\nT7: Solution treatment followed by artificial overaging (525°C for 8 hours + water quenching at 60°C + aging at 185°C for 5 hours)\nbal: Balance (remaining percentage is aluminum)",
